67
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:14,531
What do I need to remember?

68
00:04:15,067 --> 00:04:15,632
Image:

69
00:04:15,632 --> 00:04:17,422
read-only template.

70
00:04:17,422 --> 00:04:18,365
Container:

71
00:04:18,365 --> 00:04:22,416
image + its own writable layer + a process.

72
00:04:22,967 --> 00:04:28,706
Many containers can run from one image; their changes stay in their own writable layers.

73
00:04:29,267 --> 00:04:35,226
docker container diff shows a container's changes; docker cp copies files out.

74
00:04:36,967 --> 00:04:39,696
How does listing containers work?

75
00:04:40,233 --> 00:04:43,000
docker ps is the command you will type most often.

76
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,273
By default it lists running containers only; -a adds the stopped ones.

77
00:04:49,273 --> 00:04:54,547
Filters (--filter) narrow the list down, --format chooses the columns,

78
00:04:54,547 --> 00:04:58,238
and -q prints only IDs for use in other commands.

79
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,959
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

80
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,550
Running containers only:

81
00:05:06,550 --> 00:05:09,454
All containers, with their status:

82
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,722
All containers, with their status:

83
00:05:12,722 --> 00:05:19,127
Exited (0) succeeded, Exited (3) failed with exit code 3, Created never started.

84
00:05:19,127 --> 00:05:23,289
