97
00:06:13,713 --> 00:06:16,464
What do I need to remember?

98
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,034
docker ps:

99
00:06:18,034 --> 00:06:21,448
running containers; docker ps -a:

100
00:06:21,448 --> 00:06:22,689
all of them.

101
00:06:23,233 --> 00:06:31,438
--filter status=exited (not "stopped"), name=, ancestor=, exited=CODE.

102
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,474
--format picks columns; -q gives IDs for scripts.

103
00:06:38,233 --> 00:06:40,973
Why would I need container lifecycle?

104
00:06:41,533 --> 00:06:43,914
A container moves through states:

105
00:06:43,914 --> 00:06:47,809
created, running, paused, exited, and finally removed.

106
00:06:47,809 --> 00:06:51,776
Each docker command moves it from one state to another.

107
00:06:51,776 --> 00:06:54,517
The most important transition is stop:

108
00:06:54,517 --> 00:06:59,927
Docker first asks the main process to shut down cleanly (a SIGTERM signal),

109
00:06:59,927 --> 00:07:04,183
waits for a grace period, and only then kills it (SIGKILL).

110
00:07:05,933 --> 00:07:09,893
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

111
00:07:10,433 --> 00:07:13,796
Walk an Nginx container through every state:

112
00:07:13,796 --> 00:07:14,407
Stop it.

113
00:07:14,407 --> 00:07:20,368
Nginx handles the stop signal and shuts down cleanly, so it exits with code 0:

114
00:07:20,933 --> 00:07:21,574
Stop it.

115
00:07:21,574 --> 00:07:27,818
Nginx handles the stop signal and shuts down cleanly, so it exits with code 0:

116
00:07:27,818 --> 00:07:31,100
An exited container can be started again:

117
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:33,822
its writable layer is still there.

118
00:07:33,822 --> 00:07:35,743
restart is stop + start.

119
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:39,992
An exited container can be started again:

120
00:07:39,992 --> 00:07:43,053
its writable layer is still there.

121
00:07:43,053 --> 00:07:45,215
restart is stop + start.

122
00:07:45,215 --> 00:07:48,366
