00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:48,731
What do I need to remember?

247
00:16:49,267 --> 00:16:51,666
Each container is independent:

248
00:16:51,666 --> 00:16:55,106
its own process, files and published ports.

249
00:16:55,667 --> 00:17:02,071
Labels group containers; --filter label=… with -q acts on the whole group.

250
00:17:02,633 --> 00:17:05,490
$(docker ps -q …) can be empty:

251
00:17:05,490 --> 00:17:09,268
guard commands that need at least one ID.

252
00:17:11,033 --> 00:17:13,593
Why would I need container logs?

253
00:17:14,133 --> 00:17:25,180
Docker captures everything a container's main process writes to standard output (stdout) and standard error (stderr) and keeps it as the container's log.

254
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:29,439
docker logs reads it, even after the container has stopped.

255
00:17:29,439 --> 00:17:34,349
This only works if the application writes its logs to stdout/stderr:

256
00:17:34,349 --> 00:17:39,259
logs written to a file inside the container are invisible to Docker.

257
00:17:39,259 --> 00:17:44,818
That is why official images such as Nginx redirect their log files to stdout.

258
00:17:46,567 --> 00:17:52,746
A container that writes some lines to stdout and an error to stderr, then exits:

259
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:57,303
The container has already exited, but its log is still there:

260
00:17:57,303 --> 00:18:02,751
The error may appear before the last order line although the program wrote it last:

261
00:18:02,751 --> 00:18:09,707
stdout and stderr are two separate streams, and Docker does not guarantee the order of lines between them.

262
00:18:09,707 --> 00:18:12,530
Within one stream the order is always kept.

263
00:18:13,067 --> 00:18:18,535
